Executor-service, and future.

Raw thread left us with three problems: threads are unbounded and expensive, they return no result, and you manage their lifecycle by hand. The executor-service solves all three. It's the standard way to run concurrent work in Java: you stop creating threads, and start submitting tasks to a pool that owns the threads for you.

Here's the shift. An executor-service is a thread pool — a fixed set of worker threads, and a queue of pending tasks. You hand it work; it runs each task on a free worker, reusing threads instead of spawning one per task. So executors-dot-new-fixed-thread-pool of four gives you four reusable workers. Then pool-dot-submit, of a task, hands off the work. The pool decouples how many tasks you have from how many threads exist. Ten thousand tasks submitted to a four-thread pool run four at a time — no memory blow-up, because there are only ever four threads.

Now, how do you get a result back? Runnable returns nothing. When a task must produce a value, use a callable. A callable is like a runnable, but its call method returns a value — and it may throw a checked exception. Submitting a callable gives you a future — a handle to a result that isn't ready yet. So future-of-integer f equals pool-dot-submit, of a lambda that computes an expensive sum. That returns immediately. You can do other work while it computes. Then f-dot-get blocks the calling thread until the value is ready — or it throws an execution-exception, wrapping whatever the task threw; that's the cause-chaining from module eight. So a future is a promise: the answer will be here; ask for it when you need it.

Beyond get, a future lets you check and control the task. F-dot-is-done tells you, without blocking, whether it's finished. F-dot-get, with a timeout of two seconds, waits — but gives up after the timeout, throwing a timeout-exception. And f-dot-cancel, of true, attempts to stop it, interrupting it if it's running. Get with a timeout is the disciplined way to wait — because an unbounded get can hang your thread forever if the task never completes.

There's a lifecycle rule: you must shut it down. A pool's threads keep the java virtual machine alive, so an executor-service must be closed, or the program never exits. Modern Java makes this clean — executor-service is auto-closeable, from module eight, so a try-with-resources shuts it down and waits for tasks to finish. So: try, open-paren, executor-service pool equals new-fixed-thread-pool of four, close-paren, then submit your tasks. And close waits for the submitted tasks, then shuts the pool down. Before Java twenty-one, you called shutdown, and then await-termination, by hand — the try-with-resources does both.

Why is this the real baseline? Because the executor-service fixes raw thread's every flaw. It's bounded — the pool caps thread count and cost. It's result-bearing — through callable and future. And it's managed — submit and forget, with clean shutdown. It also sets up the two directions the rest of the module goes. When you have many tasks, and the old worry was "too many threads", Java twenty-one's virtual threads, coming next, change the calculus entirely. And when you want to combine asynchronous results, rather than block on get, completable-future picks up where future stops. Future is the primitive; both are its evolution.
